The bug is in the condition:

```js
if (input !== null && input !== undefined && input !== false)
```

It only rejects `null`, `undefined`, and `false`. An empty string `""` is not equal to any of those values, so it passes the check and the function returns `true`.

Fix: explicitly reject empty strings.

```js
function validateInput(input) {
    if (input !== null && input !== undefined && input !== false && input !== "") {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}
```

<solution language="javascript" verdict="fix">
function validateInput(input) {
    if (input !== null && input !== undefined && input !== false && input !== "") {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}
</solution>

