message.content:
The bug is a data race in `Increment()`:

```go
c.count++
```

`c.count++` is not atomic in Go. It is effectively:

1. read `c.count`
2. add `1`
3. write `c.count`

When many goroutines do this concurrently, two or more goroutines can read the same value, add `1`, and write back the same result. That causes lost updates, so the final count is lower than expected.

`GetCount()` is also unsafe if it can be called concurrently with `Increment()` in your service, even though in this example it is called after `wg.Wait()`.

The fix is to make the counter access synchronized. The simplest fix is to use `sync/atomic`.

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
	"sync/atomic"
)

type Counter struct {
	count int64
}

func (c *Counter) Increment() {
	atomic.AddInt64(&c.count, 1)
}

func (c *Counter) GetCount() int64 {
	return atomic.LoadInt64(&c.count)
}

func main() {
	counter := &Counter{}
	var wg sync.WaitGroup

	for i := 0; i < 1000; i++ {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func() {
			defer wg.Done()
			counter.Increment()
		}()
	}

	wg.Wait()
	fmt.Println("Final count:", counter.GetCount())
}
</solution>

